    "case_id": "51",
    "case_summary": "A 13-year-old girl presented with rapidly worsening paralysis and altered mental status. She had experienced a mild illness resembling a viral infection the previous week, accompanied by lethargy. In the days leading up to her presentation, she developed back pain and difficulty walking. On the day of admission, she experienced a headache, vomiting, fluctuating consciousness, and became unable to move her limbs. Neurological examination revealed an altered mental state, paralysis of all four limbs, absent deep tendon and abdominal reflexes, and mild bilateral facial weakness. A high thoracic (C4) sensory level, severe urinary retention, and stool incontinence were noted. Systemic examination showed no fever, rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or signs of sepsis. Her past medical history included acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), common B-cell variant, diagnosed at age 2. Six months after completing chemotherapy, she experienced a relapse involving the central nervous system and bone marrow, leading to encephalopathy. She received cranial irradiation and additional chemotherapy, achieving remission. Due to the high risk of further relapses, she underwent a bone marrow transplant (BMT) from a matched unrelated male donor, with total body irradiation as part of the conditioning regimen. She remained in prolonged remission for the following 7 years without any noticeable CNS or systemic issues.\n\nInitial MR imaging showed diffuse involvement of the CNS. The spinal cord was diffusely swollen showing central cord T2 hyperintensity from C1 to the conus. There were multifocal grey and white matter lesions in the cerebral cortex, subcortical areas and cerebellum which showed diffuse gadolinium enhancement. CSF examination revealed 125 white cells with a lymphoblastic appearance and 5 red cells. CSF protein (0.61 g/l) and CSF:blood lactate ratio (3.2:1.2 mmol/l) were raised with a low CSF: glucose ratio (4.3:8.9 mmol/l). CSF cytospin showed increased proportion of lymphocytes. Immunophenotyping of the CSF cells demonstrated the cells were CD10 negative, strongly CD2 and CD7 positive and terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT) negative. These findings suggested that the cells were mature T-cells. Cytogenetic studies and FISH using centromeric X and Y-chromosome markers on the CSF lymphocytes confirmed that the overwhelming majority of cells were of male donor type (6–14% of cells were of host origin but not clonal).\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
